{
 "name": "Surgery: The Lymphatic System and Spleen",
 "questions": [
  {
   "stem": "A 62-year-old man is admitted to the hospital for treatment of septic shock secondary to pyelonephritis. A central venous catheter is placed in the left internal jugular vein after two attempts. A chest radiograph obtained immediately afterward shows the catheter tip in the superior vena cava and no pneumothorax. Enteral tube feeding is begun that evening. The following morning, he has increasing dyspnea. His temperature is 37.4°C (99.3°F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 108/66 mm Hg. Breath sounds are decreased over the left lung base. A repeat chest radiograph shows opacification of the left hemithorax. Thoracentesis yields 700 mL of milky fluid with a triglyceride concentration of 260 mg/dL. Gram stain of the fluid shows no organisms.\n\nInjury to which of the following structures is the most likely cause of these findings?",
   "choices": [
    "A. Azygos vein",
    "B. Cisterna chyli",
    "C. Left brachiocephalic vein",
    "D. Right lymphatic duct",
    "E. Thoracic duct"
   ],
   "correct": 4,
   "explanation": "The thoracic duct terminates at the posterior wall of the venous confluence formed by the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins, placing it directly in the path of a left internal jugular needle and dilator. Needle or dilator laceration → chyle escapes into the left pleural space → chylothorax. The duct is only 2–3 mm in caliber, so the leak is initially slow and the immediate radiograph is normal; enteral fat then markedly increases chyle flow → overnight accumulation → opacified hemithorax by morning. Milky fluid with a triglyceride concentration above 110 mg/dL and no organisms confirms chyle rather than empyema or hemothorax.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "0": "Azygos vein injury would have to occur during a right-sided posterior mediastinal procedure, and it would produce a bloody effusion with a falling hematocrit — the milky, triglyceride-rich fluid excludes it.",
    "1": "The cisterna chyli lies retroperitoneally at L1–L2; injury there requires a retroperitoneal or aortic operation and produces chylous ascites, not a left pleural collection.",
    "2": "Brachiocephalic vein laceration would give a hemothorax or mediastinal hematoma with hemodynamic deterioration and bloody thoracentesis fluid; the fluid here would have to be blood rather than chyle.",
    "3": "The right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper body and empties at the RIGHT subclavian–internal jugular junction; it would have to have been a right-sided line, and the resulting effusion would be right-sided and far smaller."
   },
   "eli5": "There is a big lymph pipe that dumps into a vein on the left side of your neck. If a needle goes in at exactly that spot, the pipe can spring a leak. Once the patient eats fat, milky fluid pours into the chest and fills it up overnight."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 54-year-old woman comes to the office because of an 8-week history of heaviness and aching of the right leg. The symptoms began approximately 6 weeks after she underwent an open right inguinal hernia repair. She describes the leg as tight but denies severe pain. She has no history of serious illness and takes no medications. She does not smoke cigarettes or drink alcoholic beverages. Her temperature is 37.1°C (98.8°F), pulse is 76/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 126/78 mm Hg. Examination shows nonpitting swelling of the entire right lower extremity with mild overlying warmth and pink discoloration; the left leg is normal. The skin of the second interdigital web space of the right foot cannot be tented between the examiner's fingers. There is no jugular venous distention. Leukocyte count is 7200/mm3 and serum albumin concentration is 4.2 g/dL. Duplex ultrasonography of the right lower extremity shows no evidence of deep venous thrombosis.<img src='../notes/img/lymphedema-clinical-limbs.jpg' alt='Clinical photographs of established lymphedema showing gross nonpitting swelling of a limb with thickened skin and loss of normal contour' style='display:block;max-width:100%;margin:14px auto;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.15);background:#fff'>Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this patient's findings?",
   "choices": [
    "A. Decreased plasma oncotic pressure",
    "B. Impaired lymphatic transport capacity",
    "C. Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure from deep venous obstruction",
    "D. Increased capillary permeability from bacterial infection",
    "E. Sodium and water retention from decreased cardiac output"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "explanation": "Groin dissection during inguinal hernia repair disrupts the inguinal lymphatics → lymph production exceeds transport capacity → protein and cellular metabolites accumulate in the interstitium → water follows the retained protein and collagen is deposited → nonpitting, fibrotic swelling. The inability to tent the skin of the interdigital web space is the Stemmer sign, which reflects loss of cutaneous elasticity from that protein-rich interstitial accumulation. The 6-week interval between the operation and symptom onset, and the strictly unilateral distribution, complete the picture.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "0": "Hypoalbuminemia would produce generalized, bilateral, pitting edema; the serum albumin concentration would have to be low rather than 4.2 g/dL.",
    "2": "Venous obstruction raises hydrostatic pressure but leaves protein clearance intact, giving pitting edema with a tentable web space; the duplex ultrasonogram would have to show thrombus.",
    "3": "Cellulitis would require fever, leukocytosis, and rapidly advancing tenderness — the temperature and leukocyte count here are normal, and warmth with pink discoloration is characteristic of the acute phase of lymphedema itself.",
    "4": "Heart failure produces BILATERAL dependent edema with jugular venous distention; the swelling would have to involve both legs rather than one."
   },
   "eli5": "Lymph fluid is full of protein, and only lymph vessels can carry that protein back out of the tissue. Surgery in the groin cut those vessels, so the protein got stuck and pulled water in with it. That is why the leg is puffy and the skin is too tough to pinch."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 40-year-old woman comes to the office because of a 14-month history of progressive swelling of the right arm and right breast. She emigrated from the Philippines 6 months ago. She has no pain, fever, or weight loss. She has never been pregnant. She has no history of surgery or radiation therapy. Her temperature is 37.0°C (98.6°F), pulse is 78/min, and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. Examination shows marked nonpitting swelling of the right upper extremity and thickened, dimpled skin over the right breast. No breast mass or nipple discharge is present. There is no axillary or supraclavicular lymphadenopathy. Leukocyte count is 9800/mm3 with 18% eosinophils. Mammography of the right breast shows no malignancy.\n\nWhich of the following infectious agents is the most likely cause of these findings?",
   "choices": [
    "A. Loa loa",
    "B. Onchocerca volvulus",
    "C. Schistosoma haematobium",
    "D. Trichinella spiralis",
    "E. Wuchereria bancrofti"
   ],
   "correct": 4,
   "explanation": "Wuchereria bancrofti is the most frequent cause of secondary lymphedema worldwide and is endemic in the Philippines. Adult worms lodge within lymphatic channels → chronic lymphatic obstruction and fibrosis → progressive nonpitting swelling of a limb, breast, or genitalia (elephantiasis). The peripheral eosinophilia of 18% points to helminthic infection, and the negative mammogram excludes the malignant lymphatic obstruction that the peau d'orange appearance would otherwise suggest. Unlike surgical lymphedema, the parasitic form is treatable with antihelminthic medication.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "0": "Loa loa causes transient migratory subcutaneous (Calabar) swellings and subconjunctival worm migration; the swelling would have to be episodic and migratory rather than fixed and progressive over 14 months.",
    "1": "Onchocerca volvulus produces subcutaneous nodules, pruritic dermatitis, and keratitis; the presentation would have to include visual impairment and a nodular rash rather than isolated lymphatic obstruction.",
    "2": "Schistosoma haematobium involves the vesical venous plexus and causes hematuria and bladder squamous cell carcinoma; the complaint would have to be urinary rather than a swollen arm.",
    "3": "Trichinella spiralis causes myositis with periorbital edema and myalgia after ingestion of undercooked pork; the edema would have to be periorbital and accompanied by muscle pain and elevated creatine kinase activity."
   },
   "eli5": "A tiny worm spread by mosquitoes can crawl into the lymph pipes and block them. The fluid then has nowhere to drain, so the arm swells up huge and stays that way. High eosinophils are the body's clue that a parasite is involved."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 6-week-old infant born at 28 weeks' gestation is evaluated in the neonatal intensive care unit because of two episodes of gram-negative bacteremia during the past 3 weeks. At 2 weeks of age he underwent ileocecal resection for necrotizing enterocolitis, with removal of the terminal ileum and cecum. He is now receiving full enteral feeds. His temperature is 38.3°C (100.9°F), pulse is 168/min, and respirations are 52/min. Examination shows a soft, nondistended abdomen with a well-healed incision. Serum IgG and IgM concentrations are within the reference ranges for age, and lymphocyte subset analysis shows normal CD4+ and CD8+ counts. Blood cultures have grown Escherichia coli and Klebsiella pneumoniae.<img src='../notes/img/peyers-patch-structure-mcells.jpg' alt='Diagram of a Peyer patch showing intestinal lumen, villi, an inductive site with an M cell, follicle with germinal center, lamina propria, submucosa, and muscle layer' style='display:block;max-width:100%;margin:14px auto;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.15);background:#fff'>Decreased production of which of the following best explains this infant's susceptibility to infection?",
   "choices": [
    "A. IgA",
    "B. IgD",
    "C. IgE",
    "D. IgG",
    "E. IgM"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "explanation": "Peyer patches are concentrated in the ileum and are the principal site of secretory IgA production. Antigen is sampled from the lumen by microfold (M) cells → passed to underlying lymphoid tissue → antigen-sensitized B cells become IgA-secreting plasma cells → dimeric IgA is secreted onto the mucosal surface, where it acts as “mucosal antiseptic paint” that prevents bacterial adherence and invasion. Ileocecal resection removes this tissue → loss of mucosal IgA → unopposed bacterial translocation of enteric gram-negative organisms across the mucosa → recurrent bacteremia. The normal serum IgG and IgM concentrations and normal T-lymphocyte subsets localize the defect specifically to mucosal immunity.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "1": "IgD functions as a B-lymphocyte surface receptor and has no established secretory or mucosal barrier role; its deficiency does not produce recurrent bacteremia.",
    "2": "IgE mediates mast cell degranulation in allergy and antiparasitic responses; deficiency would have to present with impaired helminth defense, not translocation of enteric gram-negative bacteria.",
    "3": "IgG is the principal serum opsonin, and its deficiency causes recurrent sinopulmonary infection with encapsulated organisms — the serum IgG concentration would have to be low rather than normal for age.",
    "4": "IgM is produced chiefly in the spleen and mediates early complement-fixing responses; the serum IgM concentration would have to be low, and the surgical history would have to be splenectomy rather than ileocecal resection."
   },
   "eli5": "The last part of the small intestine makes a special antibody that coats the gut like paint and keeps bacteria from sneaking through. Surgeons removed that part of his bowel to save his life. Now the paint is missing, so gut bacteria slip into his blood."
  },
  {
   "stem": "An investigator is studying lymphocyte maturation using thymic tissue obtained from infants during cardiac surgery. Immature lymphocytes are fluorescently labeled and their movement through the thymic lobule is tracked over 96 hours. Cells located in the outer region of the lobule are observed to contact epithelial cells displaying self-peptide–MHC complexes. Cells that engage these complexes with adequate affinity survive and migrate toward the center of the lobule; those that fail to engage them undergo programmed cell death and are cleared by resident macrophages.\n\nWhich of the following processes is being observed in the surviving cells?",
   "choices": [
    "A. Affinity maturation",
    "B. Clonal anergy",
    "C. Isotype switching",
    "D. Negative selection",
    "E. Positive selection"
   ],
   "correct": 4,
   "explanation": "Immature T lymphocytes first reside in the thymic CORTEX, the outer region of the lobule, where they contact antigen-presenting epithelial cells bearing self-peptide–MHC complexes. Thymocytes that bind with adequate affinity are rescued from death and mature → migrate inward to the medulla; those that fail to bind die by apoptosis and are cleared by macrophages. Survival contingent on successful engagement defines positive selection — selection FOR the ability to recognize self-MHC.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "0": "Affinity maturation is somatic hypermutation of immunoglobulin genes in germinal centers of peripheral lymphoid tissue; it would have to be occurring in B lymphocytes in a lymph node follicle, not thymocytes in the thymic cortex.",
    "1": "Clonal anergy renders a self-reactive lymphocyte functionally unresponsive but ALIVE; the cells failing engagement here are dying by apoptosis rather than persisting in an inactivated state.",
    "2": "Isotype switching changes the immunoglobulin heavy-chain constant region in B lymphocytes after antigen exposure; it does not occur in T lymphocytes and does not determine thymocyte survival.",
    "3": "Negative selection would be observed if cells binding self-peptide–MHC with HIGH affinity were the ones deleted; here it is the cells that fail to engage that die, which is the opposite direction, and negative selection occurs in the medulla rather than the cortex."
   },
   "eli5": "Baby immune cells go to school in the thymus and have to pass a test: can you recognize the body's own name tags? Cells that can are allowed to graduate and move deeper inside. Cells that cannot are told to self-destruct and get cleaned up."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 34-year-old man undergoes emergency splenectomy for a grade IV splenic laceration sustained in a motor vehicle collision. A closed suction drain is left in the left upper quadrant. On postoperative day 3, he has fever and left upper quadrant pain. His temperature is 38.6°C (101.5°F), pulse is 102/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 122/74 mm Hg. Examination shows tenderness to palpation of the left upper quadrant. The drain has produced 180 mL of clear fluid during the past 24 hours. Analysis of the drain fluid shows an amylase activity of 4200 U/L. Leukocyte count is 14,300/mm3. Test of the drain fluid for occult blood is negative.\n\nIntraoperative injury to which of the following structures is the most likely cause of these findings?",
   "choices": [
    "A. Greater curvature of the stomach",
    "B. Left adrenal gland",
    "C. Splenic flexure of the colon",
    "D. Tail of the pancreas",
    "E. Upper pole of the left kidney"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "explanation": "The tail of the pancreas lies medial to the spleen and extends to within millimeters of the splenic hilum. Blind application of a vascular clamp across the hilum → transection or crush injury of the pancreatic tail → pancreatic ductal disruption → leakage of amylase-rich pancreatic fluid into the splenic bed. Drain fluid amylase activity many times the serum value with clear, blood-negative fluid establishes a pancreatic leak. This is why careful dissection of the hilum, rather than blind clamping, is the second step of the operation.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "0": "Gastric injury from short gastric vessel ligation produces a leak of enteric contents with peritonitis and drain fluid containing bile or particulate matter; the drain fluid would have to be enteric rather than amylase-rich and clear.",
    "1": "The left adrenal gland lies posteromedial to the spleen, and its injury produces bleeding or, if bilateral, adrenal insufficiency with hypotension and hyponatremia — not an amylase-rich drain output.",
    "2": "Splenic flexure injury during mobilization would cause a colonic leak with feculent drainage and diffuse peritonitis; the drain fluid would have to be feculent and the patient far more toxic.",
    "4": "Renal injury would produce drainage with a creatinine concentration far exceeding that of serum, along with hematuria; the fluid would have to be urine, and it would test positive for blood."
   },
   "eli5": "The tail of the pancreas sits right up against the spleen, almost touching it. If the surgeon clamps blindly, that tail gets pinched and starts leaking digestive juice. The drain fluid gives it away because it is loaded with an enzyme called amylase."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 28-year-old woman undergoes splenectomy for immune thrombocytopenic purpura refractory to corticosteroid therapy. Six weeks postoperatively she is asymptomatic and her platelet count has normalized. A peripheral blood smear obtained at the follow-up visit shows nucleated erythrocytes, target cells, and erythrocytes containing small, round, densely basophilic nuclear remnants. Her hemoglobin concentration is 13.1 g/dL and leukocyte count is 9100/mm3.<img src='../notes/img/spleen-histology-slide.jpg' alt='Histologic section of spleen showing the capsule, trabeculae, nodules of white pulp, and surrounding red pulp' style='display:block;max-width:100%;margin:14px auto;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.15);background:#fff'>Loss of the function normally performed by which of the following is the most likely cause of these findings?",
   "choices": [
    "A. Cords and sinusoids of the red pulp",
    "B. Germinal centers of the white pulp",
    "C. Marginal zone",
    "D. Periarteriolar lymphoid sheath",
    "E. Splenic capsule and trabeculae"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "explanation": "The red pulp constitutes approximately 75% of splenic volume and consists of reticular cords interposed with venous sinusoids. Erythrocytes squeeze from the cords through narrow interendothelial slits into the sinusoids, and macrophages lining the cords pit inclusions and remove senescent or deformed cells → nuclear remnants (Howell-Jolly bodies), nucleated erythrocytes, and target cells are normally cleared before entering the circulation. Splenectomy removes this filtration bed → these abnormal forms persist on the smear. Their presence is the expected morphologic signature of an absent or nonfunctioning spleen.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "1": "Germinal centers support B-lymphocyte proliferation and antibody affinity maturation; their loss impairs humoral responses to encapsulated organisms, which would present as infection rather than as retained erythrocyte inclusions.",
    "2": "The marginal zone contains macrophages and antibody-generating B cells at the interface between red and white pulp; loss impairs early antibody responses to polysaccharide antigens, not the mechanical pitting of erythrocyte inclusions.",
    "3": "The periarteriolar lymphoid sheath is the T-lymphocyte zone surrounding the central arteriole; its loss would impair cell-mediated immunity and would have to present with impaired responses to intracellular pathogens.",
    "4": "The capsule and trabeculae are the dense connective tissue framework that partitions the pulp and is the source of pain with splenic distention; they perform no filtration function and their loss produces no smear abnormality."
   },
   "eli5": "The spleen works like an oil filter, squeezing blood cells through tight spaces and picking the junk out of them. Once the spleen is gone, nothing filters that junk anymore. So leftover bits inside red blood cells now show up under the microscope."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 42-year-old man comes to the office for a routine health maintenance examination. He underwent splenectomy 3 years ago for traumatic splenic rupture. He reports feeling well. He takes no medications. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows a well-healed midline abdominal scar and no other abnormalities. Hemoglobin concentration is 14.6 g/dL, leukocyte count is 8400/mm3, and platelet count is 388,000/mm3. Serum IgG and IgA concentrations are within the reference range. His physician counsels him that he remains at lifelong risk for fulminant infection with Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis, and Haemophilus influenzae.\n\nProduction of which of the following is most likely decreased in this patient?",
   "choices": [
    "A. C3b",
    "B. IgA",
    "C. IgG",
    "D. IgM",
    "E. Properdin"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "explanation": "The spleen is the major site of IgM production. Encapsulated bacteria resist ordinary phagocytosis because their polysaccharide capsule conceals surface antigens → they must first be opsonized by antibody and complement before macrophages can ingest them, and IgM is the principal early complement-fixing opsonizing antibody against polysaccharide antigens. Splenectomy removes both the dominant source of IgM AND the filtration bed where opsonized organisms are cleared → loss of both halves of the system at once → susceptibility specifically to encapsulated organisms.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "0": "C3b is synthesized principally by hepatocytes, and its production is unaffected by splenectomy; deficiency would have to accompany hepatic failure or an inherited complement deficiency and would broaden susceptibility beyond encapsulated organisms.",
    "1": "IgA is produced by mucosal plasma cells in Peyer patches and other MALT, not the spleen — the measured serum IgA concentration here is normal, and its loss would follow ileocecal resection instead.",
    "2": "IgG is produced by long-lived plasma cells residing chiefly in the bone marrow, which is preserved after splenectomy; the measured serum IgG concentration is normal.",
    "4": "Properdin stabilizes the alternative-pathway C3 convertase and is made by leukocytes; inherited properdin deficiency selectively predisposes to Neisseria species, and it would have to explain meningococcal infection alone rather than the full encapsulated triad."
   },
   "eli5": "Some bacteria wear a slippery coat so immune cells cannot grab them. The spleen makes the antibody that sticks to that coat and gives the immune cells a handle. Without a spleen, the handle is missing and those bacteria can multiply out of control."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 48-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of two episodes of hematemesis during the past 4 hours. He has a 3-year history of chronic pancreatitis related to alcohol use; a CT scan 6 months ago showed a pancreatic pseudocyst posterior to the body of the pancreas. His pulse is 112/min and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. Examination shows pallor and a spleen palpated 6 cm below the left costal margin. There is no jaundice, ascites, palmar erythema, or asterixis. Upper endoscopy shows large varices confined to the gastric fundus; the esophagus appears normal.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin              9.4 g/dL\nLeukocyte count         3,100/mm3\nPlatelet count          84,000/mm3\nProthrombin time        11.8 sec\nSerum albumin           4.1 g/dL\nTotal bilirubin         0.8 mg/dL\nAST                     26 U/L\nALT                     22 U/L\n<img src='../notes/img/amboss-portal-venous-drainage.jpg' alt='Diagram of the portal venous system showing the hepatic portal vein, splenic vein, superior and inferior mesenteric veins, short gastric, left and right gastric, gastroepiploic, pancreatic, and esophageal tributaries' style='display:block;max-width:100%;margin:14px auto;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.15);background:#fff'>Thrombosis of which of the following vessels is the most likely cause of this patient's bleeding?",
   "choices": [
    "A. Hepatic portal vein",
    "B. Inferior mesenteric vein",
    "C. Left gastric vein",
    "D. Splenic vein",
    "E. Superior mesenteric vein"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "explanation": "The splenic vein courses along the posterior and inferior border of the pancreas and is a low-pressure vessel, so pancreatic inflammation or a pseudocyst compresses it long before it troubles the higher-pressure splenic artery → splenic vein thrombosis. Two consequences follow: outflow obstruction of the spleen → congestive splenomegaly → hypersplenism with pancytopenia; and diversion of short gastric and gastroepiploic flow through the gastric submucosa → varices confined to the gastric fundus. Isolated GASTRIC varices with entirely NORMAL synthetic liver function (albumin 4.1 g/dL, normal prothrombin time and bilirubin) is the discriminator from cirrhotic portal hypertension, which also produces esophageal varices. This entity is the one in which splenectomy is curative.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "0": "Portal vein thrombosis raises pressure throughout the portal system and produces BOTH esophageal and gastric varices, usually with ascites; the endoscopy would have to show esophageal varices as well.",
    "1": "The inferior mesenteric vein drains the hindgut, and its obstruction produces left-sided colonic ischemia or rectal varices; the bleeding would have to be from the lower gastrointestinal tract.",
    "2": "The left gastric vein is the outflow tract that becomes an esophagogastric collateral in generalized portal hypertension; isolated thrombosis of it would relieve rather than produce fundal varices, and it would not account for the splenomegaly and pancytopenia.",
    "4": "Superior mesenteric vein thrombosis presents with pain out of proportion to examination, bowel wall edema, and mesenteric ischemia; the patient would have to have severe abdominal pain and bowel compromise rather than isolated fundal varices."
   },
   "eli5": "The vein draining the spleen runs right behind the pancreas, and an angry pancreas squeezes it shut. Blood backs up, so the spleen swells and eats blood cells, and stomach veins balloon up and bleed. Because the liver is perfectly healthy here, the problem has to be that one vein."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 57-year-old man with a 12-year history of alcohol-related cirrhosis comes to the office for follow-up. He has fatigue but no bleeding. His pulse is 84/min and blood pressure is 104/68 mm Hg. Examination shows scleral icterus, spider angiomas, shifting dullness, and a spleen palpated 8 cm below the left costal margin. Upper endoscopy performed 1 week ago showed both esophageal and gastric varices. Hemoglobin concentration is 9.8 g/dL, leukocyte count is 2,600/mm3, and platelet count is 62,000/mm3. Serum albumin concentration is 2.8 g/dL, total bilirubin concentration is 2.4 mg/dL, and international normalized ratio is 1.6. Abdominal ultrasonography shows a nodular liver, a patent portal vein, and splenomegaly.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate management of this patient's splenomegaly and cytopenias?",
   "choices": [
    "A. Coil embolization of the splenic artery",
    "B. Nonselective beta-blocker therapy with endoscopic surveillance",
    "C. Partial splenectomy",
    "D. Platelet transfusion followed by splenectomy",
    "E. Splenectomy"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "explanation": "Portal hypertension is the most common cause of secondary hypersplenism, and in it the spleen is an innocent bystander: the obstruction to flow lies upstream in the cirrhotic liver and persists after the spleen is removed. Congestion → splenic sequestration → pancytopenia, but removing the sequestering organ does not lower portal pressure and exposes a coagulopathic patient to major operative hemorrhage and to lifelong postsplenectomy sepsis risk. Management is therefore directed at the portal hypertension itself → nonselective beta-blockade to reduce portal inflow, with endoscopic surveillance and band ligation of varices. The patent portal vein and the presence of esophageal as well as gastric varices confirm generalized portal hypertension rather than isolated splenic vein thrombosis.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "0": "Splenic artery coil embolization is used preoperatively 24 hours before a planned elective splenectomy for a pathologically enlarged spleen to reduce operative blood loss; it would have to be an adjunct to a planned operation rather than definitive therapy.",
    "2": "Partial splenectomy is used for splenic cysts and focal lesions where preservation of splenic function is the goal; it does not address portal pressure and would have to be a localized splenic lesion rather than congestive splenomegaly.",
    "3": "Platelet transfusion in hypersplenism is rapidly consumed by the sequestering spleen and is reserved for active bleeding or an imminent procedure; the patient would have to be actively bleeding, and it does not make splenectomy appropriate.",
    "4": "Splenectomy would be curative if the obstruction were confined to the splenic vein — the patient would have to have isolated gastric varices with normal synthetic liver function and a thrombosed splenic vein rather than cirrhosis with a patent portal vein."
   },
   "eli5": "The spleen is swollen because the scarred liver is damming up blood behind it. Taking out the spleen does not fix the dam, and this patient bleeds easily anyway. So you treat the pressure problem instead and keep an eye on the veins that might burst."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 9-year-old girl is brought to the office by her mother because of a 6-month history of intermittent yellowing of the eyes and fatigue. She has had two episodes of right upper quadrant pain after meals during the past month. Her father underwent splenectomy at 22 years of age for an unspecified blood disorder. Her temperature is 37.0°C (98.6°F), pulse is 92/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Examination shows scleral icterus and a spleen palpated 4 cm below the left costal margin.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin                  9.6 g/dL\nMean corpuscular volume     84 um3\nMean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration   37.2 g/dL (N=32–36)\nReticulocyte count          9%\nTotal bilirubin             3.4 mg/dL\nDirect bilirubin            0.4 mg/dL\n\nA peripheral blood smear shows numerous small, round erythrocytes lacking central pallor. Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test result is negative. Abdominal ultrasonography shows multiple gallstones.\n\nA defect in which of the following proteins is the most likely cause of this patient's findings?",
   "choices": [
    "A. ADAMTS13",
    "B. Beta-globin chain",
    "C. Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase",
    "D. Pyruvate kinase",
    "E. Spectrin"
   ],
   "correct": 4,
   "explanation": "Hereditary spherocytosis is an autosomal dominant dysfunction or deficiency of spectrin, a cytoskeletal protein anchoring the erythrocyte membrane. Loss of vertical membrane linkage → membrane blebbing and surface area loss → cells assume a spherical shape with increased mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration → rigid spherocytes cannot traverse the splenic cords → extravascular destruction in the red pulp → chronic hemolysis with reticulocytosis, unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, splenomegaly, and pigment gallstones. The negative direct antiglobulin test excludes an immune cause, and the affected father establishes autosomal dominant transmission. Splenectomy is curative and is deliberately delayed past 4 years of age to limit the risk of overwhelming postsplenectomy sepsis.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "0": "ADAMTS13 deficiency causes thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura; the smear would have to show schistocytes with thrombocytopenia, neurologic changes, and renal impairment rather than spherocytes with a normal platelet count.",
    "1": "A beta-globin chain defect produces thalassemia or sickle cell disease, with microcytosis and target cells or sickled forms; the mean corpuscular volume would have to be markedly low with a normal or reduced mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration.",
    "2": "Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency is X-linked and causes episodic hemolysis after oxidant exposure, with bite cells and Heinz bodies; the pattern would have to be episodic in a male patient following an oxidant trigger.",
    "3": "Pyruvate kinase deficiency is autosomal recessive and produces chronic nonspherocytic hemolytic anemia with echinocytes; the family history would have to show unaffected consanguineous parents rather than an affected father."
   },
   "eli5": "Red blood cells need a stretchy skeleton to squeeze through the spleen's tiny gaps. Hers is broken, so the cells turn into little balls that get stuck and destroyed. The leftover pigment builds up and forms gallstones and yellow eyes."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 34-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of confusion and fever for 2 days. Her husband reports that she has been increasingly forgetful and had a brief episode of slurred speech this morning. She takes no medications. Her temperature is 38.7°C (101.7°F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 138/86 mm Hg. She is oriented to person only. Examination shows scattered petechiae over the lower extremities and scleral icterus. There is no splenomegaly and no lymphadenopathy.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin              7.9 g/dL\nPlatelet count          18,000/mm3\nReticulocyte count      8%\nSerum creatinine        2.8 mg/dL\nTotal bilirubin         3.1 mg/dL\nLactate dehydrogenase   1,240 U/L\nProthrombin time        12.1 sec\nPartial thromboplastin time  29 sec\nFibrinogen              340 mg/dL\n\nA peripheral blood smear shows fragmented erythrocytes and nucleated erythrocytes. Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test result is negative.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
   "choices": [
    "A. Corticosteroid therapy alone",
    "B. Plasma exchange",
    "C. Platelet transfusion",
    "D. Rituximab therapy",
    "E. Splenectomy"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "explanation": "Fever, thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia with schistocytes, neurologic changes, and renal insufficiency constitute the pentad of thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura. Deficient ADAMTS13 activity → failure to cleave ultralarge von Willebrand factor multimers → platelet-rich microthrombi in the microvasculature → mechanical shearing of erythrocytes and consumption of platelets. The normal prothrombin time, partial thromboplastin time, and fibrinogen concentration exclude disseminated intravascular coagulation, and the negative direct antiglobulin test excludes autoimmune hemolysis. Plasma exchange removes autoantibody and replaces ADAMTS13 and is first-line therapy; untreated, the disease is rapidly progressive and usually fatal.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "0": "Corticosteroids are adjunctive in thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura but never used alone; they would be the correct sole first-line therapy if the disorder were autoimmune hemolytic anemia, which would require a POSITIVE direct antiglobulin test.",
    "2": "Platelet transfusion supplies substrate for further microthrombus formation and may precipitate neurologic deterioration; it would be appropriate only if the thrombocytopenia were from failed production or if life-threatening hemorrhage were present.",
    "3": "Rituximab is reserved for relapsed or refractory disease after plasma exchange has been initiated; it would have to be a second-line decision after failure of exchange rather than the initial step.",
    "4": "Splenectomy is a salvage measure for patients who relapse or require repeated plasma exchange; for it to be first-line the disorder would have to be immune thrombocytopenic purpura refractory to medical therapy, which lacks schistocytes, fever, and renal failure."
   },
   "eli5": "Tiny clots are forming all through her small blood vessels, shredding red cells and using up platelets. Giving platelets just feeds the clots and makes it worse. Swapping out her plasma removes the cause and replaces the missing enzyme."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 29-year-old woman comes to the office because of easy bruising and gum bleeding for 2 weeks. She underwent laparoscopic splenectomy 8 months ago for immune thrombocytopenic purpura refractory to corticosteroid therapy; her platelet count rose from 19,000/mm3 preoperatively to 210,000/mm3 by 6 weeks postoperatively and remained normal at her 4-month visit. She takes no medications. Vital signs are within normal limits. Examination shows scattered petechiae over both shins and no hepatosplenomegaly. Platelet count is now 22,000/mm3; hemoglobin concentration and leukocyte count are within the reference ranges. A peripheral blood smear shows reduced platelets with occasional large forms; no Howell-Jolly bodies are identified. Bone marrow examination shows increased megakaryocytes.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next step in diagnosis?",
   "choices": [
    "A. Bone marrow cytogenetic analysis",
    "B. Direct antiglobulin test",
    "C. Radionuclide scintigraphy with heat-damaged erythrocytes",
    "D. Repeat platelet count in 4 weeks",
    "E. Serum antiplatelet antibody assay"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "explanation": "Accessory splenic tissue is present in approximately 20% of the population, and more than 80% of accessory spleens lie near the splenic hilum. Because that tissue is functional, an accessory spleen missed at the original operation hypertrophies over months → resumption of antibody-mediated platelet destruction → relapse of immune thrombocytopenic purpura after an initially successful splenectomy. The decisive buried clue is the ABSENCE of Howell-Jolly bodies: after a complete splenectomy these inclusions should appear permanently on the smear, so their absence indicates functioning splenic tissue remains somewhere. Radionuclide scintigraphy with heat-damaged erythrocytes is the study used to locate residual or accessory splenic tissue.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "0": "Cytogenetic analysis screens for a clonal marrow disorder such as myelodysplastic syndrome; the marrow would have to show dysplasia or hypocellularity rather than the increased megakaryocytes of peripheral destruction.",
    "1": "The direct antiglobulin test detects antibody bound to ERYTHROCYTES and is used for autoimmune hemolytic anemia or Evans syndrome; the patient would have to have anemia with reticulocytosis rather than an isolated fall in platelets.",
    "3": "Observation is appropriate for asymptomatic mild thrombocytopenia, but this patient has a platelet count of 22,000/mm3 with mucocutaneous bleeding, and a documented cause is retrievable now.",
    "4": "Antiplatelet antibody assays lack sensitivity and specificity and do not change management; the diagnosis of immune thrombocytopenic purpura is already established, and the question is where the destruction is occurring."
   },
   "eli5": "About one in five people have a small spare spleen hiding near the main one. If the surgeon misses it, that spare grows and starts destroying platelets all over again. The giveaway is that her blood cells still look filtered, which means spleen tissue is still working somewhere."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 38-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of fever and left upper quadrant pain for 5 days. He injects heroin intravenously several times weekly. His temperature is 39.1°C (102.4°F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 104/62 mm Hg. Examination shows track marks over both antecubital fossae and tenderness to palpation of the left upper quadrant with guarding. No murmur is heard. Leukocyte count is 19,800/mm3 with 88% segmented neutrophils. Two sets of blood cultures have grown Staphylococcus aureus. CT scan of the abdomen with contrast shows a 7-cm rim-enhancing collection within the splenic parenchyma containing multiple foci of gas.<img src='../notes/img/splenic-abscess-ct.jpg' alt='Axial CT scan of the upper abdomen showing a splenic collection containing multiple foci of gas' style='display:block;max-width:100%;margin:14px auto;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.15);background:#fff'>The gas within the splenic collection is most likely a direct indication of which of the following?",
   "choices": [
    "A. Gas-forming bacterial infection within the abscess cavity",
    "B. Communication between the abscess and the colonic lumen",
    "C. Necrosis of the splenic parenchyma from arterial embolization",
    "D. Prior percutaneous instrumentation of the collection",
    "E. Rupture of an underlying splenic artery pseudoaneurysm"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "explanation": "Gas within a solid organ is produced by fermenting, gas-forming bacteria and marks a severe, often anaerobic or mixed infection. Hematogenous seeding of the spleen in an injection drug user → bacterial proliferation within infarcted or devitalized parenchyma → metabolic gas production within the abscess cavity. The clinical rule is that air in a solid organ signifies a bad infection requiring prompt source control; splenic abscess carries a mortality of 40–100%, most commonly from Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus species, and treatment is broad-spectrum antibiotics with splenectomy or, increasingly, percutaneous drainage.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "1": "An enteric-splenic fistula would require a contiguous colonic process such as diverticulitis or malignancy eroding into the spleen, and the CT scan would have to show a tract between bowel and the collection along with feculent contents.",
    "2": "Bland infarction produces a wedge-shaped, nonenhancing hypodensity WITHOUT gas; gas appears only once organisms colonize the infarcted tissue, so infarction alone cannot account for it.",
    "3": "Iatrogenic air would require a documented prior drainage or aspiration procedure; this patient has had no instrumentation of the collection.",
    "4": "A ruptured pseudoaneurysm produces active contrast extravasation and hemorrhage with hemodynamic collapse and a falling hematocrit, not intraparenchymal gas."
   },
   "eli5": "Some bacteria actually make gas as they grow, like yeast in bread dough. Seeing gas bubbles inside a solid organ means those bacteria are thriving in there. That is a dangerous infection that needs draining or surgery fast."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 17-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department 40 minutes after he fell onto his left side while skateboarding. He has had sore throat, fever, and fatigue for the past 3 weeks and was advised to avoid contact sports. His temperature is 37.4°C (99.3°F), pulse is 128/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 84/52 mm Hg. Examination shows pallor, posterior cervical lymphadenopathy, and tenderness to palpation of the left upper quadrant. He also reports pain at the tip of the left shoulder; the shoulder itself is nontender and moves through a full range of motion, and there is no deformity or crepitus. A heterophile antibody test result is positive. Focused abdominal ultrasonography shows free fluid in the left upper quadrant and pelvis.<img src='../notes/img/splenic-rupture-ct.jpg' alt='Axial CT scan of the abdomen showing splenic injury with surrounding perisplenic fluid' style='display:block;max-width:100%;margin:14px auto;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.15);background:#fff'>Which of the following best explains this patient's shoulder pain?",
   "choices": [
    "A. Compression of the left brachial plexus by an expanding hematoma",
    "B. Irritation of the diaphragm with referral through the phrenic nerve",
    "C. Ischemia of the shoulder girdle musculature from hypoperfusion",
    "D. Stretch of the splenic capsule transmitted along splenic afferent fibers",
    "E. Traction on the left sympathetic chain by displaced abdominal viscera"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "explanation": "Infectious mononucleosis produces a large, soft, friable spleen that ruptures with minimal trauma. Rupture → hemoperitoneum collecting beneath the left hemidiaphragm → blood irritates the diaphragmatic peritoneum, which is innervated by the phrenic nerve arising from C3–C5 → afferent signals converge on C3–C5 dermatomes in the spinal cord → pain is perceived at the tip of the ipsilateral shoulder (Kehr sign). The normal shoulder examination confirms the pain is referred rather than the result of local injury.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "0": "Brachial plexus compression would cause motor weakness and dermatomal sensory loss in the arm, and it would have to follow a supraclavicular or axillary injury rather than an intraperitoneal hemorrhage.",
    "2": "Muscular ischemia from hypoperfusion causes diffuse, symmetric pain in dependent muscle groups; the pain would have to be bilateral and accompanied by an elevated creatine kinase activity rather than localized to one shoulder tip.",
    "3": "Splenic capsular stretch is transmitted along afferents entering at T6–T10 and is felt as left upper quadrant pain, not shoulder pain; for capsular stretch to be the answer the pain would have to remain localized to the abdomen.",
    "4": "Sympathetic chain traction produces autonomic disturbances such as Horner syndrome, not somatic pain referred to a specific dermatome."
   },
   "eli5": "The nerve that supplies the diaphragm comes from the same neck levels that supply your shoulder skin. When blood from the burst spleen pools under the diaphragm, the brain gets confused about where the signal came from. So it feels like the shoulder hurts even though nothing is wrong there."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 34-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department by her husband because of fever, myalgia, and rhinorrhea that began 6 hours earlier. She underwent emergency splenectomy 14 months ago for a shattered spleen sustained in a motor vehicle collision; her recovery was uncomplicated and she was discharged on postoperative day 4. She takes no medications and has received no vaccinations since the operation. On arrival her temperature is 39.4°C (102.9°F), pulse is 136/min, respirations are 30/min, and blood pressure is 76/44 mm Hg. She is somnolent. Examination shows mottled extremities and rapidly spreading confluent purpura over the trunk and limbs. Leukocyte count is 1,800/mm3, platelet count is 34,000/mm3, and fibrinogen concentration is 88 mg/dL.<img src='../notes/img/opsi-purpura-fulminans.jpg' alt='Clinical photographs showing progressive purpura and peripheral gangrene of the hands and legs' style='display:block;max-width:100%;margin:14px auto;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.15);background:#fff'>Administration of vaccines against encapsulated organisms would have been most appropriate at which of the following times in this patient's course?",
   "choices": [
    "A. Two weeks before the operation",
    "B. Immediately before the induction of anesthesia",
    "C. During the first 24 hours after the operation",
    "D. Before discharge from the hospital",
    "E. At the 6-week postoperative visit"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "explanation": "Vaccination against Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis, and Haemophilus influenzae is the central preventive measure after splenectomy, and its timing depends on whether the operation was planned. This splenectomy was performed emergently for trauma, so the 2-week preoperative window was never available. Vaccines are therefore given before hospital discharge — specifically once the patient is recovering rather than acutely ill, because an acutely injured or hemorrhaging patient has a downregulated immune system that will not mount an adequate antibody response. Giving them before discharge also captures the patient while still in the system; the risk of overwhelming postsplenectomy infection is greatest within the first 2 years, exactly the window in which this patient presented.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "0": "Two weeks preoperatively is correct for an ELECTIVE splenectomy, when the spleen is still present and the immune system intact; this operation would have to have been planned rather than performed emergently after trauma.",
    "1": "Vaccination at induction leaves no time to mount a response before the spleen is removed, and the perioperative stress response blunts antibody production; the timing would have to precede the operation by approximately 2 weeks to be effective.",
    "2": "During the first 24 hours the patient is in the acute phase of hemorrhagic and surgical stress with a downregulated immune response, so seroconversion is poor; the patient would have to be in the recovery phase for the vaccine to take.",
    "4": "Deferring to an outpatient visit leaves the patient unprotected through the highest-risk early period and depends on follow-up that frequently fails; it would be acceptable only for the scheduled revaccination interval of every 3 to 5 years thereafter."
   },
   "eli5": "Without a spleen, three specific bacteria can kill you in a single day. Vaccines teach the body to fight them, but they only work if given when the body is strong enough to learn. After emergency surgery, that moment is right before the patient goes home."
  },
  {
   "stem": "A 58-year-old woman undergoes elective laparoscopic left hemicolectomy for a benign adenoma. During mobilization of the splenic flexure, retraction avulses a portion of the splenic capsule, and bleeding is not controlled by packing or topical hemostatic agents. The procedure is converted to an open laparotomy and splenectomy is performed. Estimated blood loss is 900 mL. On the first postoperative day the patient is hemodynamically stable and alert. She asks the surgeon why her abdominal incision is much larger than she was told to expect and why she has been told she will need vaccinations.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate initial response by the physician?",
   "choices": [
    "A. “During the operation your spleen was injured by a retractor and had to be removed; I want to explain what happened and what it means for you.”",
    "B. “Some bleeding occurred and we made an adjustment during surgery; there is really nothing for you to worry about now.”",
    "C. “The spleen was removed because it was abnormal and would likely have caused you problems later on.”",
    "D. “This is a recognized complication that can occur in any abdominal operation, so it is not really anyone's fault.”",
    "E. “Your family asked me to wait until they arrive before going over the details, so let us discuss it this afternoon.”"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "explanation": "The patient has capacity, is asking directly, and is entitled to know what happened to her. Disclosure of an iatrogenic injury requires a truthful, specific account of the event followed by an explanation of its consequences → the response names the injury, names the mechanism, and opens the conversation about what asplenia will mean, including vaccination and lifelong infection risk. Acknowledging the error plainly also preserves the therapeutic relationship and satisfies the professional obligation to identify and own an intraoperative complication rather than obscure it.",
   "wrongExplanations": {
    "1": "This violates truthfulness through minimization and false reassurance; a vague “adjustment” conceals a permanent organ loss the patient has directly asked about.",
    "2": "This is a factual misrepresentation — the spleen was normal until it was injured — and fabricating a medical indication to cover a complication is deception.",
    "3": "This deflects responsibility by generalizing to all operations before the patient has even been told what occurred; framing the discussion around fault avoidance places the physician's interests ahead of the patient's need for information.",
    "4": "This subordinates a competent patient's right to her own medical information to the family's preferences; the patient, not her relatives, controls the disclosure of her clinical course."
   },
   "eli5": "The surgeon accidentally damaged her spleen and had to take it out. She asked a direct question, so she deserves a straight, honest answer about what happened. Hiding it or blaming bad luck would break her trust and leave her unprepared for what comes next."
  }
 ]
}